===Alternative forms===
* {{alter|en|hooka|hooker|houka|hukka|hukkah|huqqa|huqqah}}

===Etymology===
From {{bor|en|hi|हुक़्क़ा|sc=ur-Arab}} and {{bor|en|ur|حقہ|tr=huqqā|sc=ur-Arab}}, in turn, derived from {{der|en|ar|حُقَّة||pot, jar}}, from {{m|ar|حُقّ||cavity, hollow}}.<ref>{{cite web |title=hookah |url=https://www.etymonline.com/search?q=hookah&utm_campaign=sd&utm_medium=serp&utm_source=ds_search |publisher=Online Etymology Dictionary |date=2022 |quote=hookah (n.) also hooka, 1763, via Hindi or Persian or directly from Arabic huqqah "small box, vessel" (through which the smoke is drawn), related to huqq "a hollow place."}}</ref><ref name="TCALR1916">{{cite book|title=The Ceylon Antiquary and Literary Register, Volume 1|year=1916|work=[[The Times]]|page=111|quote=It has even drawn largely on English, and such words as daktar and platfarm, isteshan and tikat, trem-ghari and rel-ghari, registran karna and apil karna are as common as similar words are in Ceylon. To make up for it Hindustani has not only enriched the vocabulary of Anglo-Indian English with such words as topi and pugre, oheerot and hookah, dhoby and sepoy, ghary and tamasha, durbar and bukshish, Kachcheri and Punkah, but has contributed to it words like jungle, bazar, [and] loot.}}</ref>
{{root|en|ar|ح ق ق}}

